# --- Wave 5 item 13 (notes/wave5-worklist/13-compile-longtail.md) ---------
# `compile.other` longtail residual after the structural fixes in this
# item (`ScalaRunTime.toObjectArray`/`stringOf`, `Collections.min`/`max`/
# `addAll`-erasure, `Runtime.totalMemory`, `Thread.yield`, and the
# cross-package `report.echo` change). Five fixtures need their own
# follow-up structural work and are excluded with reason tags.
Parser.scala                                # compile-encoder-edge (lambda hoisted out of a lazy-val initializer with a `_$`-prefixed wildcard-param name; def is missing from emitted .pyir while the call site references the fully expanded `Parser_package_____strToToken_lzyINIT1__anonfun_1`. Needs LambdaLift/MoveStatics interaction in GenPython.)
enum-values.scala                           # jvm-reflection-not-supported (uses `reflect.Selectable.reflectiveSelectable`'s `applyDynamic` which goes through `Class.getMethod`)
for-desugar-strawman.scala                  # compile-encoder-edge-empty-package (top-level extension method `applyFilter`/`mapDefined` in a colon-block `xs.flatMapDefined: x => ... is.applyFilter(...) { ... }` resolves owner to `<empty>` and emits `Unresolved module class '_lessempty_greater'`)
i14198.scala                                # gc-semantic-dependency (`terminally(ref.get().get() == null)` polls until the JVM GC reclaims a `WeakReference`; CPython's reference-counting + cycle GC has different reclamation timing, so the loop never terminates)
i7843.scala                                 # jvm-concurrent (uses `ForkJoinPool`, `Thread.UncaughtExceptionHandler`, `ExecutionException(String, Throwable)`, `StackOverflowError(String)` — same shape as the existing `jvm-only` cluster in item 07)

# --- Wave 5 item 05 (notes/wave5-worklist/05-assert-genuine-semantic.md) -
# Genuine Scala-vs-Python semantic gaps. The NaN equality sub-shape was
# fixed in `library-py BoxesRunTime.equals` (skip the `eq` shortcut for
# Python `float`s so generic `==` honours IEEE-754 `NaN != NaN`); the
# fixtures below remain blocked by separate, deeper structural gaps that
# fall outside this item's surgical scope.

# nan-instance-method-dispatch: `Double.NaN.isNaN` lowers to
# `boxedDouble.isNaN__Z()`. After `Predef.double2Double`, `boxedDouble`
# is the same Python `float`, which has no `isNaN__Z` attribute. The
# structural fix is to teach GenPython to special-case `isNaN`/`isInfinite`
# on raw-Python receivers (analogous to `_scpy_get_class` for `Object.getClass`)
# or to teach the runtime to dispatch instance-method calls on primitive
# floats through the registered `java.lang.Float`/`java.lang.Double`
# class. Either approach is wider than the NaN-equality fix and is left
# for a follow-up.
blame_eye_triple_eee-double.scala           # nan-instance-method-dispatch (`isNaN__Z` on raw Python float; first 8 lines now match check)
blame_eye_triple_eee-float.scala            # nan-instance-method-dispatch (`isNaN__Z` on raw Python float; first 8 lines now match check)

# python-lambda-class-identity-not-supported: Scala `() => x` lifts to a
# distinct synthetic class on the JVM; LambdaMetafactory may share the
# class for identical literals, but two lambdas with different captures
# always have distinct `getClass`. Python closures and SAM bridges all
# share `<class 'function'>` (or `_scpy_FnN`), so `lam1.getClass ==
# lam2.getClass` collapses to `true` regardless of the source. Without
# synthesizing a wrapper class per lambda site (which fights closure
# capture identity and Python serialization), this is a fundamental
# JVM-vs-Python semantic gap.
getclass.scala                              # python-lambda-class-identity-not-supported (asserts `f.getClass.toString` matches `class Test$$$Lambda$`; Python closure has no JVM-shaped class name)
lambda-serialization-security.scala         # python-lambda-class-identity-not-supported (asserts `reconstituted1.getClass == reconstituted2.getClass` after pickle round-trip; Python `function` class identity not preserved)

# explicit-boxed-number-not-stored: pylib `java.lang.Double(value)` /
# `java.lang.Float(value)` / `java.lang.Long(value)` constructors are
# empty bodies — the underlying primitive is recovered via
# `this.asInstanceOf[scala.Double]`, which works for the Predef
# `double2Double` implicit (boxing is identity in Python) but *not*
# for explicit `new java.lang.Double(d)`, which mints an `_scpy_Object`
# instance whose Python type is `java_lang_Double`, not `float`.
# `doubleValue()` on that instance returns the instance itself,
# which then propagates into `_scpy_float_to_str` and trips
# `math.isnan`. Structural fix is either to store the value in an
# instance attribute (e.g. `_scpy_value`) and have `doubleValue()`
# read it back, or to teach the runtime to unwrap `java_lang_*`
# proxies in primitive-typed call sites — both wider than item 05.
numbereq.scala                              # explicit-boxed-number-not-stored (`new java.lang.Double(x.toDouble)` paths; pylib boxes don't round-trip through `doubleValue()`)
